     <span class="section-marker">Section 1 of four</span>
     <h2>How this landscape is organized</h2>
     <p>This is the accessibility work of a learning design practice, gathered in one place. It draws on two branches: Accessibility, which concerns access at the level of an individual person and the obligation an institution holds toward them, and Universal Design for Learning, which concerns designing for the predictable range of learners before any individual request is made. The two are related but not the same, and the arrangement here keeps them distinct while showing where they meet.</p>
-    <p>Resources are arranged in seven groups by what they are for, rather than by which branch they grow on. A group therefore holds resources from both branches where the question they answer is shared. Each resource names its home branch so the distinction is never lost. Groups are given in alphabetical order, followed by the three engagements the resources ground.</p>
+    <p>Resources are arranged in seven groups by what they are for, rather than by which branch they grow on. A group therefore holds resources from both branches where the question they answer is shared. Each resource names its home branch so the distinction is never lost. Groups are given in alphabetical order, followed by the three engagements that draw on them.</p>
     <p>Every resource states the bodies of knowledge it rests on and cites its sources by number to the reference list in Section 4. Every resource on this page is practised; where it is marked as in development, the grounding and sources are settled and the written resource is not yet published.</p>
     <p>This landscape zooms into two branches of the learning design practice mapped at <a href="https://www.andrearobin.com/landscape">andrearobin.com</a>, where the trunk is Groundwork, the learning design method. The contributions here that do not appear on that map are made possible by <a href="https://www.accessiblelearninglabs.com/fieldwork">Fieldwork</a>, the research method practised at Accessible Learning Labs.</p>
     <p>Two kinds of relationship are given for each resource. <strong>Most related</strong> is calculated from sources and themes held in common, and surfaces resources that answer similar questions from different groups. <strong>Connected resources</strong> are the direct relationships recorded on the map — the resources a reader is likely to need alongside this one.</p>
